(defclass datamunge:rbf-gaussian-process-surrogate (datamunge:bayesian-surrogate) ()
  (:documentation "Exact Gaussian-process regression surrogate with the squared-exponential kernel

`s\\exp(-\\lVert x-x'\\rVert^2/2\\ell^2)`.

fit() Cholesky-factors `K+\\sigma_n^2I=LL^\\top` once and solves for
`\\alpha=(K+\\sigma_n^2I)^{-1}(y-m)`; predict() then needs one forward substitution per point:
mean `m+k_*^\\top\\alpha` and variance `s-\\lVert L^{-1}k_*\\rVert^2`.

The two-argument constructor keeps the original fixed setup: zero prior mean, unit signal
variance, no normalisation or fitting, expected improvement. The options constructor adds
value normalisation, marginal-likelihood fitting of the hyperparameters and other acquisitions.
The kernel is isotropic, so the inputs should be on comparable scales.

**Example**

```cpp
dm::optim::RBFGaussianProcessSurrogate gp(1.0, 0.0);
gp.fit({{0.0}, {2.0}}, {1.0, -1.0});
const auto p = gp.predict({1.0});
std::print(\"{:.4f} {:.4f}\\n\", p.mean, p.sd);  // 0.0000 0.5933
```

**References**

- C. E. Rasmussen and C. K. I. Williams (2006). *Gaussian Processes for Machine Learning*,
  chapters 2 and 5. MIT Press.

See also: BayesianOptimization, expected_improvement()

Constructors:
  (make-rbf-gaussian-process-surrogate &optional (length-scale 1d0) (noise 1d-6)) → rbf-gaussian-process-surrogate
  (make-rbf-gaussian-process-surrogate options) → rbf-gaussian-process-surrogate

Subclass it in Lisp; methods of the subclass override the C++ virtual methods:
  (defclass my-rbf-gaussian-process-surrogate (datamunge:rbf-gaussian-process-surrogate) ())
  (datamunge:define-override datamunge:fit ((self my-rbf-gaussian-process-surrogate) points values) ...)
  (make-instance 'my-rbf-gaussian-process-surrogate :args (list ...constructor arguments...))
May override (CALL-NEXT-METHOD runs the C++ one): fit, acquisition."))

