(defclass datamunge:bspline (datamunge:native-object) ()
  (:documentation "B-spline design factory, analogous to R's splines::bs().
Owns a clamped basis on fixed boundaries, reused for training and prediction.
By default the first basis column is omitted, so a formula's intercept does
not duplicate the partition of unity. No fitting or smoothing is performed.
Const evaluations are independent and safe to run concurrently.

**Example**

```cpp
const dm::Bspline basis(0, 1, {0, 0.5, 1});
const auto design = basis.eval({0, 0.5, 1});
std::print(\"{} {}\\n\", design.rows(), design.cols()); // 3 4
```

**References**

- C. de Boor (1972). \"On calculating with B-splines\". Journal of Approximation
  Theory 6(1), 50-62. doi:10.1016/0021-9045(72)90080-9.

See also: bs(), fda::BSplineBasis, stats::LM

Constructors:
  (make-bspline lower upper knots &optional (degree 3) (intercept nil)) → bspline"))

(defclass datamunge:b-spline-regression (datamunge:native-object) ()
  (:documentation "A least-squares B-spline regression curve y ≈ Σ_j β_j B_j(x). `dm::BSR` for short.

The fit is ordinary least squares (no roughness penalty). Throws std::invalid_argument for
mismatched/empty inputs or too few basis functions for the degree, and std::runtime_error when
the design is rank deficient (some basis function has too little data under it; use fewer
basis functions / knots).

Constructors:
  (make-b-spline-regression x y n-basis &optional (degree 3)) → b-spline-regression
  (make-b-spline-regression x y knots &optional (degree 3)) → b-spline-regression"))

(defclass datamunge:p-spline-regression (datamunge:native-object) ()
  (:documentation "A P-spline regression curve y ≈ Σ_j β_j B_j(x) (Eilers & Marx). `dm::PSR` for short.

Minimizes ||y - Bβ||² + λ ||D_d β||², where D_d is the order-d difference matrix on the
coefficients (d = 2 by default). Uniform knots continue past both ends of the data
without repeated boundary knots. When d <= degree+1, polynomials of degree < d
go unpenalized: λ → ∞ gives the least-squares line for d = 2. A rich basis can
include functions with no data under them if the data identify the penalty nullspace.
Without an explicit λ, it is chosen
by minimizing generalized cross-validation, GCV(λ) = n·RSS / (n − edf)².

The polynomial-nullspace claim requires uniform spacing; it need not hold for irregular
supplied knots. A positive lambda identifies a rich basis only when the design identifies
the penalty nullspace. No values are dropped. The class owns its fit and permits concurrent
const reads. Sparse assembly and dense Cholesky cost O(n(p+1)^2+K^3); GCV repeats
this work. Very large lambda can make the equations numerically singular.

**Example**

```cpp
const dm::PSR fit({0,1,2},{0,1,0},3,1.0,1,2);
std::print(\"{:.6f} {:.6f}\\n\",fit.predict({1})[0],fit.effective_df()); // 0.428571 2.142857
```

**References**

- P. H. C. Eilers and B. D. Marx (1996). Flexible smoothing with B-splines and penalties.
  https://doi.org/10.1214/ss/1038425655

See also: fda::fit_pspline(), smoothing_path(), summary()

Constructors:
  (make-p-spline-regression x y n-basis) → p-spline-regression
  (make-p-spline-regression x y n-basis lambda &optional (degree 3) (penalty-order 2)) → p-spline-regression
  (make-p-spline-regression x y knots) → p-spline-regression
  (make-p-spline-regression x y knots lambda &optional (degree 3) (penalty-order 2)) → p-spline-regression"))

